{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat assignments/T-SPECM-spec-expert-union.md'","aggregated_output":"# T-SPECM — Expert-union model for speculative decoding on offloaded MoE\nQuestion: when d drafted tokens are verified in one batch, how many DISTINCT experts per layer are touched (vs d*top_k), and what decode speedup does the T-PL4 bandwidth model predict as a function of draft length d and acceptance rate a, per CPU-offload K?\nInputs: real Qwen routing traces artifacts/codexQRT/{prose,chat,code}.jsonl (see artifacts/codexQRT/ANALYSIS.md for format), cc/moe_planner/decode_model.py + calibration (T-PL4), decisions/D042 §1 (measured: ngram-mod 2.4x rewrite / 3.0x repeat at K=30, acceptance ~100%, mean draft len unknown; base 35.6 t/s).\nWrite ONLY cc/spec_model/** and artifacts/codexSPECM/**.\nDeliver: (1) union curves E[distinct experts per layer] for d=1..16 from consecutive DECODE tokens in each trace; (2) predicted tokens/s = accepted tokens per step / step time, where step time uses CPU bytes scaled by union(d)/top_k, GPU bytes similarly, plus fixed per-step costs from PL4; table for K in {0? if fits,18,30,42,48}, d in {1,2,3,4,8,16}, a in {0.5,0.8,1.0}; (3) check the model against D042's measured 2.4x/3.0x (state what mean draft length would be implied); (4) a recommended `spec` lever rule for the planner (when to enable, which draft max). pytest tests. Final message: key tables, validation vs D042, rule.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
